        "id": "different-questions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different tools answer different questions",
        "content": "A useful starting point for digital travel in Iran is to separate discovering a destination from understanding it and reaching it. ToIran presents city-based destination information alongside commercial itineraries. OpenStreetMap supplies geographic data maintained by contributors. UNESCO's World Heritage interface organizes places through heritage designations and geographic filters. These are complementary approaches: a destination article explains why somewhere might interest a reader, while a map establishes spatial relationships and a heritage record explains the basis of recognition. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nThis distinction matters when planning beyond a familiar city name. A description can help identify a subject worth exploring, but its presence online does not establish that an entrance is open, a route is usable, or an activity is operating. The editorial approach used here therefore treats digital resources as identifiable sources with particular functions, rather than combining them into a single claim of verified travel readiness.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "destination-information",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Destination discovery and commercial context",
        "content": "ToIran illustrates an English-language approach to organizing Iranian destinations. Its city directory includes places such as Isfahan, Shiraz, Yazd, Qeshm, Chabahar, and Sanandaj. The same website offers tailored journeys and tour packages, and describes its application as drawing on the experience of guides and experts. Those statements establish how the publisher presents its service; they do not independently demonstrate the completeness of its listings or the reliability of its recommendations. [s1]\n\nFor readers, the practical distinction is between descriptive information and a transaction. An article about a city, an itinerary offered for sale, and a confirmed booking are different kinds of material even when they appear on the same platform. This page does not infer current app-store availability, payment compatibility, or booking performance from the existence of a website.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "maps-navigation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Geographic data and navigation",
        "content": "OpenStreetMap describes a worldwide mapping community whose contributors maintain features including roads, trails, cafés, and railway stations. Its Iran project page documents country-specific mapping activity and links to local conventions. This establishes an Iranian contribution context, but it is not a measured assessment of map completeness across the country. [s2][s4]\n\nApplications add further functions to geographic information. OsmAnd, for example, documents offline maps, saved places, geographic-coordinate search, and alternative place-name displays. These capabilities show how map data can become a travel interface. They should not be read as evidence that every Iranian attraction has an accurate English label or that every mapped route has been checked on the ground. Product documentation establishes documented functions; local performance requires separate examination. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "heritage-discovery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Heritage discovery at a wider scale",
        "content": "UNESCO's interactive World Heritage map provides filters for countries, regions, inscription dates, themes, and property categories. It also offers a choice concerning the display of component parts. This is particularly useful for understanding that a heritage designation may involve multiple locations rather than one visitor destination. The interface also links to UNESCO's Sites Navigator, described as a geographic information system for designated sites. [s3]\n\nFor an Iran-focused reader, such tools support a different task from street navigation: locating recognized heritage within a wider cultural or natural landscape. A heritage designation, a mapped location, and a visitor entrance should remain distinct in an itinerary. The map interface inspected for this article does not establish ticketing arrangements, operating hours, or physical accessibility. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-technology",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading digital claims carefully",
        "content": "The strongest digital travel resource makes its purpose and evidence visible. Destination platforms should be assessed as publications and service providers; community maps as maintained geographic databases; and heritage portals as institutional documentation. The examples above demonstrate those different roles without establishing a national ranking of platforms or a uniform level of coverage. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nWithin this guide, the branches of digital travel follow the same distinction. Smart travel guides concern the relationship between interpretation and location; tourism maps concern spatial representation. Museum augmented reality, virtual tours, attraction discovery, and destination management are separate subjects because a technology's ability to display information does not by itself establish an implemented service. The useful conclusion is to follow the information back to its publisher and ask exactly which part of a proposed visit it supports.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
