    "seo": {
      "title": "Smart Travel Guides for Iran: Content, Location and Offline Use",
      "description": "How digital guides connect destination articles, saved places and navigation, and what their documentation does and does not establish for Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What makes a guide useful",
        "content": "For this guide, a smart travel guide means a resource that connects explanatory writing with places a reader can locate, save, or use in a route. The important question is what the connection provides. A historical explanation, a mapped attraction, and a calculated route are separate pieces of information, even when an application presents them together. OsmAnd's documentation provides a concrete example of this combination through articles, map points, bookmarks, and route planning. [s1]\n\nFor an international reader exploring Iran, that distinction encourages a more careful way of reading. An attractive description may establish a reason to investigate a place; it does not independently establish the conditions for visiting. This article explains documented guide functions rather than certifying a particular application for travel throughout Iran.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "content-provenance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Follow the writing to its source",
        "content": "OsmAnd states that its travel guides draw on Wikivoyage, a collaboratively written travel project. Its documentation describes downloadable articles and the ability to associate places from those articles with the map. This is an example of community travel writing being delivered inside a navigation environment. It should not be mistaken for an official statement by the institution described in an article. [s1]\n\nIran-specific editorial platforms can use a different model. ToIran says its application draws on guides' and experts' experience and organizes information city by city. That is the publisher's account of its content production, not an independent accuracy audit. The difference between community authorship and a commercial publisher's editorial selection helps explain why two digital guides may frame the same destination differently. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "offline-components",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Offline reading and offline routing",
        "content": "Downloading an article and downloading the geographic information required for navigation are distinct preparations. OsmAnd's travel-guide documentation describes saving guide material for use without a mobile connection. Its route-preparation documentation separately instructs users to download the relevant maps before offline navigation. It also distinguishes routing profiles, including walking and driving. [s1][s3]\n\nThe practical implication is to examine each part of an intended workflow separately: whether the explanation is readable, whether the destination is located, and whether the chosen navigation mode can calculate the intended journey. These are editorial evaluation criteria inferred from the documented separation of guide and navigation functions; they are not results of a field test in Iran. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-development",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An established idea with changing implementations",
        "content": "OsmAnd announced its travel-guide feature in June 2018, describing Wikivoyage-based articles and downloadable guide files. That dated announcement establishes a documented stage in the product's development. It does not date the creation of smart travel guides as a whole, establish an Iranian launch, or prove that an old interface remains unchanged. [s4]\n\nA useful guide therefore needs more than a feature label. Its writing should have an identifiable origin, and its location and navigation functions should be assessed on their own terms. For Iran, the sources inspected here support an explanation of those functions, but not claims about nationwide uptake, consistent destination coverage, or the superiority of one service.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
