    "seo": {
      "title": "Tourism Maps of Iran: Reading Locations, Routes and Heritage",
      "description": "Understand community mapping, heritage interfaces, place-name displays and the limits of mapped information when exploring Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "map-purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the map's purpose first",
        "content": "A tourism map is a selection of geographic information made for a particular purpose. A database of roads and local amenities answers different questions from an institutional map of heritage properties. OpenStreetMap describes contributor-maintained information about features such as roads, trails, cafés, and stations, whereas UNESCO's heritage map offers filters built around designated properties. Neither should be treated as a complete account of a visitor's experience. [s1][s2]\n\nFor Iran, an effective reading begins by asking what a symbol represents: a named place, a transport feature, a heritage property, or something else. A marker is useful evidence of a mapped location, but its meaning depends on the dataset and interface that produced it.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "community-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Community mapping in Iran",
        "content": "The OpenStreetMap Wiki has an Iran project page documenting mapping activity and country-specific conventions. It directs contributors toward both local tagging rules and the wider project's standards. This matters because a shared map is also a structured database: how a feature is classified affects how applications can search for it or display it. The project page establishes an organized mapping context, not a nationwide completeness score. [s3]\n\nOpenStreetMap's general documentation explains that contributors use local knowledge, aerial imagery, GPS devices, and field maps. These methods describe how information can enter the database; they do not establish when a particular Iranian feature was last surveyed. For travel interpretation, provenance at the individual-location level remains a separate question. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "institutional-maps",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Heritage maps and institutional authority",
        "content": "UNESCO's World Heritage interface supports geographic discovery and distinguishes property categories and component parts. Its role is to organize heritage information rather than supply a complete street-level visitor map. [s2]\n\nThe Persepolis maps page adds an important qualification about institutional hosting. UNESCO states that responsibility for nomination-file content rests with the submitting State Party, and that publication does not imply UNESCO's endorsement of historical or legal assertions about territories and boundaries. The lesson extends to map reading: identify who prepared a document as well as who hosts it. An institutional website can make evidence accessible without assuming authorship of every statement it contains. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "names-and-reuse",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Names, display choices, and reuse",
        "content": "Map applications can also change how the same geographic information is presented. OsmAnd documents options for English, local, or phonetic place-name display. For an English-language itinerary in Iran, retaining a local-script label alongside a chosen English rendering is a useful editorial practice; availability still depends on the underlying record. The documented display option does not prove that all places have equivalent labels. [s5]\n\nMaps also carry attribution obligations. OpenStreetMap identifies its data as licensed under the Open Database License and explains that users must credit the project and make the license clear. Its documentation distinguishes requirements for browsable maps, printed maps, and static images. Anyone preparing a tourism publication should consult the requirements for the intended use rather than treating an online map as an uncredited illustration. [s6]\n\nThe most useful map is consequently one whose purpose, publisher, labels, and limitations can be understood. For an actual destination, those qualities matter more than the visual density of markers.",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
